/* WISEBYTE LESSONS · Money & Economics (batch z) */
WB.L("decimal-currency-1966", "money", "beginner", "Dollar Bill and the day Australia went decimal",
  "On one Monday in 1966, a whole nation swapped pounds, shillings and pence for dollars and cents. The government's first choice of name, the royal, was so unpopular it was dropped.",
  [
    ["Pounds, shillings and pence", "Before 1966 Australians did their sums in a system of twelves and twenties, and a 1959 committee recommended going decimal.",
      "For most of its history Australia counted money the British way. A pound was worth 20 shillings, and each shilling was worth 12 pence, so a pound held 240 pennies. Prices were written as £2 7s 6d, and totalling a shopping bill meant constant carrying of twelves and twenties. Australia had issued its own pound since 1910, but the arithmetic was inherited from medieval England. By the 1950s businesses, banks and schools were complaining. Children spent hours converting pence to shillings, and new adding machines and early computers suited tens far better. Many countries had already gone decimal, and a government committee set up in 1959 recommended Australia follow."],
    ["Royal, emu or ming?", "The government's first choice of name, the royal, was so unpopular that it was quietly dropped for the dollar.",
      "Choosing a name proved harder than the maths. The public sent in thousands of suggestions, from the austral and the kanga to the emu and the ming, a cheeky nod to Prime Minister Robert Menzies' nickname. In 1963 the government announced the new unit would be called the royal. The reaction was swift and scornful. Newspapers mocked it, and many felt it sounded old-fashioned for a young country building its own identity. Within months the government backed down and settled on the dollar, a name already used in the United States, Canada and elsewhere, and easy to say. The fuss is a reminder that money is not just maths. It carries national pride."],
    ["C-Day, 14 February 1966", "The switch happened on a single day, backed by one of Australia's biggest public education campaigns.",
      "The changeover was set for Monday 14 February 1966, nicknamed C-Day. To prepare people, the Decimal Currency Board ran one of the biggest public information campaigns Australia had seen. Its star was a cartoon character called Dollar Bill, and a catchy jingle sung to the tune of the bush ballad ‘Click Go the Shears’ explained the change on radio and television. The conversion rule was simple: ten shillings became one dollar, so one pound became two dollars. Thousands of cash registers and machines had to be converted or replaced. Old and new money circulated side by side for a while, with shopkeepers giving change in decimal currency to drain the old coins from circulation."],
    ["Animals and silver", "The new coins carried Australian wildlife, but the first round fifty cent coin held so much silver it was worth hoarding.",
      "Designer Stuart Devlin, a young Australian who later became a famous silversmith in London, put native animals on the coins instead of kings and coats of arms. The one cent showed a feathertail glider, the two cent a frilled-neck lizard, the five cent an echidna, the ten cent a lyrebird and the twenty cent a platypus. The fifty cent carried the coat of arms. The monarch stayed on the other side.\n\nOne coin did not last. The round 1966 fifty cent piece was 80 per cent silver, and as the silver price climbed, the metal became worth more than fifty cents. People hoarded it. In 1969 it was replaced by the twelve-sided copper-nickel coin still used today."],
    ["Plastic notes, vanishing coins", "Australia kept innovating, pioneering polymer banknotes and later scrapping its smallest coins.",
      "The story did not stop in 1966. In 1988, for the bicentenary of European settlement, the Reserve Bank issued a ten dollar note printed on plastic polymer instead of paper. It was developed in Australia partly to defeat counterfeiters, since a clear window and other features were very hard to copy. By 1996 Australia had become the first country with a full series of polymer notes, and the technology has since spread to many countries.\n\nMeanwhile inflation slowly shrank the value of small coins. In 1992 the one and two cent coins were withdrawn, and cash totals have been rounded to the nearest five cents ever since. Card payments are still charged to the exact cent."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why was the old pounds, shillings and pence system hard to use?", ["Twelves and twenties made adding up slow","It had no coins smaller than a shilling","Each state used different units","Prices could not be written down"], 0, "Twelves and twenties made everyday arithmetic slow, and tens suited adding machines and computers far better."],
    [1, "Why did the government drop the name ‘royal’ for the new currency?", ["Another country already used it","It was widely mocked as old-fashioned","It was too difficult to pronounce","Banks refused to put it on notes"], 1, "The name felt out of step with national identity, which shows money carries pride as well as value."],
    [2, "What was the conversion rule on C-Day?", ["One pound became one dollar","Ten shillings became one dollar","One penny became one cent","One shilling became one dollar"], 1, "The simple rule of ten shillings to a dollar made the switch easy to explain to the public."],
    [3, "Why did people hoard the original round fifty cent coin?", ["It carried the national coat of arms","Its silver was worth over fifty cents","It was only ever issued for one year","Shops were forbidden to accept it"], 1, "When a coin's metal is worth more than its face value, people keep it and spend cheaper money instead."],
    [4, "What happened to the one and two cent coins in 1992?", ["They were redesigned with new animals","They were replaced by plastic coins","They became collectors' gold coins","They were withdrawn from circulation"], 3, "Inflation shrank the value of small coins until they were no longer worth making."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"Almost nobody wanted this name for Australia's new money. Here's what we got instead."});

WB.L("exchange-rates-aussie-dollar", "money", "beginner", "Why the Aussie dollar rises and falls",
  "In 2001 an Australian dollar bought less than 50 US cents. A decade later it bought more than a US dollar. Here is what moves a currency.",
  [
    ["A price like any other", "An exchange rate is simply the price of one currency in terms of another, set by supply and demand.",
      "When you hear that the Australian dollar is ‘at 65 US cents’, it means one Australian dollar can be swapped for 65 American cents. An exchange rate is a price, and like other prices it is set by supply and demand. Every day, importers, exporters, tourists, investors and banks buy and sell currencies. Trading is enormous, measured in trillions of US dollars a day worldwide. If more people want Australian dollars, perhaps to buy Australian iron ore or bonds, the price rises. If more want to sell them, it falls. The Australian dollar, nicknamed the Aussie, is one of the most heavily traded currencies in the world."],
    ["Floating the dollar", "Australia let its currency float in December 1983, one of the biggest economic reforms in its history.",
      "It was not always this way. For decades Australia's currency was pegged, first to the British pound, then to the US dollar, and from 1974 to a basket of currencies that officials adjusted. The government and Reserve Bank effectively decided the rate, and strict controls limited how much money could flow in or out. Defending the peg became harder as money sloshed around the world. In December 1983 the Hawke government, with Treasurer Paul Keating, floated the dollar and removed most exchange controls, letting the market set the price. Since then the Reserve Bank has only occasionally stepped into the market, usually during severe disorder."],
    ["What moves the Aussie", "The Aussie tends to rise with commodity prices and with higher Australian interest rates, and to fall in global panics.",
      "Australia sells vast quantities of iron ore, coal, gas, gold, wheat and beef. When those prices climb, foreign buyers need more Australian dollars, so the currency tends to rise. That is why traders call it a commodity currency. In the 2000s, China's industrialisation lifted the dollar from under 48 US cents in 2001 to above parity in late 2010, peaking in 2011. When commodity prices later fell, so did the dollar.\n\nInterest rates matter too. If Australian rates exceed those in the United States or Japan, investors buy Australian dollars to earn more. The carry trade borrows in a low-rate currency, such as the yen, to invest in a higher-rate one. It can unwind suddenly, which is why the Aussie often falls in global crises."],
    ["Winners and losers", "A strong dollar helps importers and travellers, while a weak one helps exporters and tourism.",
      "Whether a high dollar is good news depends on who you are. When the Aussie is strong, overseas holidays are cheaper, imported cars and electronics cost less, and Australians can buy foreign shares more cheaply. But exporters suffer, because their goods become more expensive for foreign buyers, and local manufacturers struggle against cheaper imports. Universities and tourism operators also feel it, since Australia becomes a pricier place to study or visit. A weak dollar flips these effects. Overseas trips hurt, but farmers, miners and tour operators gain. This makes the exchange rate a kind of shock absorber for the economy, cushioning the blow when commodity prices or global conditions change."],
    ["The Big Mac test", "Comparing burger prices is a playful way to ask whether a currency is overvalued.",
      "If a currency is fairly priced, the same basket of goods should cost roughly the same everywhere once you convert the money. Economists call this idea purchasing power parity. In 1986 The Economist magazine turned it into a light-hearted gauge called the Big Mac index. If a Big Mac costs much more in Switzerland than in the United States after conversion, the Swiss franc looks overvalued by burger logic.\n\nThe index is deliberately simple, since local wages, rents and taxes all affect burger prices. But it captures a real idea. In the long run, exchange rates tend to drift towards levels that make prices broadly comparable, even if they stray widely in the short term."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What is an exchange rate?", ["A tax charged on overseas travel","The interest rate the central bank sets","A fixed rate set by the United Nations","The price of one currency in another"], 3, "Like any price, an exchange rate moves with supply and demand."],
    [1, "What changed when Australia floated the dollar in December 1983?", ["The market, not officials, set its price","It was pegged to the US dollar","It was linked to the price of gold","It replaced the British pound"], 0, "Floating removed the official peg and most exchange controls, so supply and demand set the rate."],
    [2, "Why does the Aussie often rise when iron ore and coal prices climb?", ["Buyers need more Australian dollars","It is backed by gold reserves","The Reserve Bank must buy the ore","Investors always prefer commodities to cash"], 0, "Higher export earnings mean more demand for Australian dollars, which is why it is called a commodity currency."],
    [3, "Who generally benefits when the Australian dollar is weak?", ["Australians holidaying overseas","Importers of foreign cars","Exporters and tourism operators","People buying foreign shares"], 2, "A weak dollar makes Australian goods and holidays cheaper for foreign buyers, but overseas purchases cost more."],
    [4, "What does the Big Mac index suggest if a burger costs far more in one country after conversion?", ["Its burgers are better quality","Its currency may be overvalued","Its wages must be falling","Its central bank has failed"], 1, "The index applies purchasing power parity: over time, exchange rates tend to drift towards levels that equalise prices."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"In 2001 it bought under 50 US cents. A decade later, more than a dollar. What changed?"});

WB.L("bank-runs", "money", "beginner", "Bank runs: when everyone wants their money at once",
  "Your bank does not keep your savings in a vault. Usually that is fine. When everyone turns up at once, it can be catastrophic.",
  [
    ["Where your money really is", "Banks lend out most deposits and keep only a fraction on hand, which makes even healthy banks fragile.",
      "When you deposit money in a bank, it does not sit in a box with your name on it. Banks take deposits that customers can withdraw at any moment and use them to fund loans that will not be repaid for years, such as mortgages and business loans. They keep only a small portion as cash and reserves, betting, sensibly, that not everyone will want their money on the same day. This arrangement is the heart of banking, turning idle savings into homes and businesses. But it creates a built-in fragility. A bank can be perfectly sound, with good loans that will be repaid, and still be unable to hand everyone their money back immediately."],
    ["The self-fulfilling panic", "A run can topple a healthy bank simply because people fear it will fall.",
      "Imagine a rumour spreads that your bank is in trouble. Even if you think it is false, you have a problem. If others believe it and withdraw their money, the bank may run out of cash, and those who wait could lose out. So the rational move is to join the queue. Everyone reasons the same way, and the fear becomes reality. Economists Douglas Diamond and Philip Dybvig described this trap in a famous 1983 model, showing a bank run can be a self-fulfilling prophecy. Their work helped earn them a share of the 2022 Nobel Prize in economics, alongside Ben Bernanke, who studied how bank failures deepened the Great Depression."],
    ["Melbourne in the 1890s", "Australia suffered one of history's great banking crises after a property bubble burst.",
      "Australia knows this story well. In the 1880s Melbourne boomed on gold wealth and borrowed British money, and land prices soared in a speculative frenzy. Dozens of building societies and land banks lent freely against property. When the bubble burst at the end of the decade, borrowers defaulted and many of these institutions collapsed. In 1893 the crisis reached the major banks. Depositors rushed to withdraw their money, and many of the colonies' major banks suspended payments within a few weeks. Some reopened after being reorganised, but savings were frozen or lost, and the colonies plunged into a deep depression that lasted much of the decade. Melbourne took years to recover."],
    ["Stopping the run", "Central banks, calm explanation and deposit insurance give savers no reason to run.",
      "In the film It's a Wonderful Life (1946), George Bailey calms panicked customers by explaining that their money is not in a safe. It is in the neighbours' houses, lent out so they could buy homes. Real banks rely on something more powerful: a central bank willing to lend them cash against good assets in an emergency, acting as a lender of last resort.\n\nThe most effective cure has been deposit insurance. After thousands of American banks failed in the early 1930s, the United States created the FDIC in 1933. Australia introduced its Financial Claims Scheme in 2008. As of the mid-2020s it protects deposits up to 250,000 dollars per account holder at each authorised institution. Because the guarantee exists, the need to use it is far less likely. Confidence itself is the protection."],
    ["Runs in the digital age", "Modern runs can happen within hours, driven by phones and social media.",
      "Runs have not disappeared. In September 2007 queues formed outside Britain's Northern Rock, one of the first visible signs of the global financial crisis and the first run on a British bank in well over a century. In March 2023 California's Silicon Valley Bank collapsed in a run of startling speed. Many customers were tech firms with deposits far above the insured limit. As worries spread through group chats and social media, they tried to pull tens of billions of dollars in a single day using banking apps. There were no queues outside, just a flood of online transfers. Regulators closed the bank within roughly two days, showing how quickly modern panic can spread."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why can a sound bank still be unable to repay all depositors at once?", ["It keeps all deposits in gold","Banks are not allowed to hold cash","Most deposits are lent out for years","The government holds the deposits"], 2, "Banks fund years-long loans with deposits that can be withdrawn at any time, so they cannot repay everyone immediately."],
    [1, "What did Diamond and Dybvig show about bank runs?", ["They only happen to badly run banks","They can be self-fulfilling, toppling even healthy banks","They are caused only by high interest rates","They cannot happen once a country has a central bank"], 1, "If enough people fear a failure and withdraw, the fear itself can cause the failure."],
    [2, "What turned Melbourne's 1880s boom into the crisis of the 1890s?", ["A property bubble that burst","A ban on British investment","A shortage of gold coins","A law forcing banks to close"], 0, "Reckless lending against rising land prices collapsed when the bubble burst, and the crisis reached the major banks in 1893."],
    [3, "What is the role of a central bank as lender of last resort?", ["Setting the price of everyday goods","Shutting down banks that lose money","Lending cash to sound banks in an emergency","Guaranteeing every loan a bank makes"], 2, "Emergency cash lets a solid bank survive a run without selling its good loans in a panic."],
    [4, "What made the 2023 run on Silicon Valley Bank so unusual?", ["It dragged on over several years","It involved only paper cheques","It was stopped by deposit insurance alone","It played out in hours, via banking apps"], 3, "Digital banking let customers move tens of billions in a day, so the run played out in hours instead of weeks."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"Your bank is not holding your savings in a vault. So what happens if everyone asks?"});

WB.L("medici-bank", "money", "beginner", "The Medici: the bankers who built a Renaissance",
  "A family of Florentine money-changers became bankers to the Pope, patrons of genius and rulers of a city. Their tricks still shape finance today.",
  [
    ["Men at the bench", "Banking in Renaissance Italy began with money-changers working at benches in the street.",
      "In the 1300s Italian cities such as Florence, Venice and Genoa were the trading hubs of Europe, awash with coins from dozens of states. Merchants needed someone to weigh, test and swap these coins, and to move money safely over long distances. Money-changers set up at tables, and the Italian word for such a bench, banco, gave us the word bank. A banker who could not pay was said to be banca rotta, a broken bench, the root of our word bankrupt. In 1397 Giovanni di Bicci de' Medici founded the Medici Bank in Florence. Within a few decades it would become the most powerful bank in Europe."],
    ["God's banker", "The Medici grew rich managing the Church's money, and avoided the ban on interest through bills of exchange.",
      "The Medici's greatest client was the papacy. The Church collected revenue from England to Poland and needed to move it to Rome without carting silver across the Alps. The Medici's network of trusted agents could do it, and for long stretches of the 1400s they were the Pope's main bankers. Later family members became popes, including Leo X and Clement VII.\n\nThere was an awkward problem. The Church condemned usury, meaning interest on a loan. The answer was the bill of exchange. A Florentine merchant handed over florins and received a document payable months later in another currency, say in Bruges or London. The rates were set so the banker profited, but on paper it was a currency exchange, not a loan. In practice, interest was hidden inside the exchange."],
    ["A multinational company", "The Medici ran semi-independent branches across Europe, an early model for modern firms.",
      "At its height the Medici Bank had branches in Rome, Venice, Milan, Geneva, Avignon, Bruges and London, among other places. Each was run by a manager who often held a share of the profits, giving him a reason to work hard, while the family in Florence kept overall control. Branches were set up as separate partnerships, so a disaster in one city would not automatically drag down the rest. The Medici and other Italian merchants also kept careful accounts using double-entry bookkeeping, recording every transaction as both a debit and a credit so errors stood out. The method was spreading among Italian traders by the 1300s, and the friar Luca Pacioli described it in print in 1494."],
    ["Money, art and power", "Medici wealth funded some of the greatest art and architecture of the Renaissance.",
      "Giovanni's son Cosimo de' Medici used the bank's fortune to become the unofficial ruler of Florence from 1434, steering politics through allies rather than holding a crown. He was also a generous patron. He funded churches, the library of the monastery of San Marco and artists such as Donatello, and supported scholars who translated ancient Greek philosophy. His grandson Lorenzo the Magnificent continued the tradition, and the young Michelangelo spent time in his household. Part of this giving was genuine love of learning and beauty. Part of it was likely an attempt to win public support, and perhaps to ease consciences troubled by the Church's teaching on wealth. Either way, Florence was transformed."],
    ["The fall of the bank", "Bad loans to rulers and weaker management brought the bank down in 1494.",
      "Under Lorenzo, who was more interested in politics and poetry than ledgers, the bank weakened. Some branch managers made reckless loans to princes, who were powerful, unpredictable and hard to sue. The London branch lent heavily to King Edward IV during the Wars of the Roses and suffered heavy losses. The Bruges branch, under Tommaso Portinari, lent to Charles the Bold, Duke of Burgundy, who was killed in battle in 1477. One by one, branches closed. In 1494, when the Medici were driven out of Florence, the bank collapsed. The lesson echoes through financial history: lending to the mighty can feel safe until the mighty decide, or are unable, to pay."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Where does the word ‘bank’ come from?", ["The Latin word for gold","The name of a German banking family","The Italian word banco, meaning bench","The banks of the river Arno"], 2, "Early money-changers worked at benches, and the Italian word for bench gave us ‘bank’."],
    [1, "How did bankers earn a return despite the Church's ban on usury?", ["By charging entry fees to churches","By building profit into bills of exchange","By selling indulgences to clients","By ignoring the ban openly"], 1, "On paper a bill of exchange was a currency swap, so the interest stayed hidden in the exchange rates."],
    [2, "Why were Medici branches set up as separate partnerships?", ["To avoid all taxes","So each branch could issue its own coins","So one branch's failure would not sink the rest","Because the Pope required it"], 2, "Separate partnerships limited the damage of any single failure, an idea modern firms still use."],
    [3, "Why did the Medici spend so much on art and scholarship?", ["The Church ordered them to","Love of learning, and a bid for public support","They had no other use for money","Artists paid them to be patrons"], 1, "Patronage mixed genuine passion with a bid for popularity, and it transformed Florence."],
    [4, "What helped bring down the Medici Bank?", ["A Florentine stock market crash","The invention of paper money","A ban on double-entry bookkeeping","Risky loans to powerful rulers"], 3, "Rulers are powerful and hard to sue, so lending to them can end in losses the lender cannot recover."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"The word bank comes from a street bench. This family turned it into an empire."});

WB.L("behavioural-economics-money", "money", "beginner", "Behavioural economics: why we're not rational with money",
  "Traditional economics imagined people as cool calculators. Then psychologists started running experiments, and the calculators turned out to be human.",
  [
    ["The rational myth", "Classic economics assumed people calculate every choice logically, but real people do not.",
      "For much of the twentieth century, economic theory leaned on a convenient character sometimes nicknamed Homo economicus. This imaginary person knows what they want, calculates costs and benefits precisely, never gets tired or tempted, and always chooses the option that serves them best. The assumption made the maths workable, and it still explains a great deal about how markets behave. But anyone who has bought something on impulse, kept a gym membership they never use or put off saving for retirement knows real people are different. Behavioural economics grew from testing how people actually decide, using experiments borrowed from psychology, and building those findings back into economics."],
    ["Losses loom larger", "Losing something tends to hurt more than gaining the same thing feels good, so people overvalue what they own.",
      "In 1979 psychologists Daniel Kahneman and Amos Tversky published prospect theory. One central idea is loss aversion: in many experiments, losing 100 dollars hurts roughly twice as much as winning 100 dollars pleases, though estimates vary and some researchers argue the effect is smaller. It helps explain why investors hang on to falling shares, and why ‘don't miss out’ marketing works. Kahneman won the 2002 Nobel Prize in economics; Tversky had died in 1996.\n\nIn a 1990 study, mugs were handed to half a class. Owners typically demanded about twice what buyers would pay. This is the endowment effect, and it explains why sellers overprice their homes and free trials work. Later research debates its strength."],
    ["Money in mental jars", "We treat money differently depending on where it came from or what we have labelled it for.",
      "In theory a dollar is a dollar. In practice we sort money into mental jars. Economist Richard Thaler called this mental accounting. Someone might carry credit card debt at a high interest rate while keeping savings in a holiday fund earning far less, because the holiday money feels untouchable. A tax refund or a win at the races often gets spent more freely than wages, as if it were not quite real money. Mental accounting is not always bad. Labelling money for bills or emergencies can stop you overspending. But it can also lead to choices that cost money, so it helps to ask occasionally whether your jars still make sense together."],
    ["Present bias", "We tend to grab rewards now and push costs into the future, even against our own plans.",
      "Ask people whether they would prefer 100 dollars in a year or 110 dollars in a year and a week, and most will wait the extra week. Ask whether they want 100 dollars today or 110 dollars next week, and many grab the money now. The gap is the same, but the present has a special pull. Economists call this present bias. It shows up in spending, saving, diets and procrastination, and it explains why buy now, pay later deals are so tempting. The future self who has to pay feels like a stranger. One response is to make good choices automatic, so your present self does not have to win the argument every time."],
    ["Nudges and super", "Small changes to how choices are presented, such as defaults, can change behaviour without banning anything.",
      "If people are predictably irrational, can choices be designed to help them? In their 2008 book Nudge, Richard Thaler and Cass Sunstein argued that defaults matter enormously. If workers are automatically enrolled in a retirement plan and must opt out, far more end up saving than if they must opt in. Thaler and Shlomo Benartzi designed Save More Tomorrow, in which workers commit in advance to saving part of future pay rises, sidestepping both present bias and loss aversion. Thaler received the Nobel Prize in 2017. Australia's compulsory superannuation, introduced in 1992, goes further than a nudge, since employers must contribute, but it rests on a similar insight about how hard saving is."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What did behavioural economics add to the idea of the perfectly rational ‘Homo economicus’?", ["Evidence from experiments on real choices","A ban on impulse purchases","Proof that markets never work","A new type of currency"], 0, "It tests real behaviour using psychology experiments, then builds the findings back into economics."],
    [1, "What is loss aversion?", ["Avoiding all investments","Preferring to save rather than spend","Feeling losses more strongly than equal gains","Fear of losing a wallet"], 2, "Losses tend to hurt more than equal gains please, which also helps explain why people overvalue what they own."],
    [2, "Someone keeps a holiday fund while paying high interest on a credit card. Which idea does this illustrate?", ["The endowment effect","Mental accounting","Present bias","Loss aversion"], 1, "Labelling money for a purpose can make it feel untouchable, even when that costs more in interest."],
    [3, "Why do buy now, pay later deals appeal so strongly?", ["They are always the cheapest option","They improve credit scores","Immediate rewards feel bigger than later costs","They are required by law"], 2, "The present has a special pull, so the cost that arrives later feels like someone else's problem."],
    [4, "According to Thaler and Sunstein, what does automatic enrolment with an opt-out do?", ["It has little effect on saving","It greatly increases the number of people saving","It forces everyone to stay in the plan","It reduces employer contributions"], 1, "Defaults matter because most people stick with the pre-set choice, so the right default helps them save."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"People often value a mug twice as highly the moment it becomes theirs. Why?"});

WB.L("gold-standard-bretton-woods", "money", "intermediate", "The gold standard and the end of Bretton Woods",
  "For generations, money was promised to be as good as gold. Then on a Sunday night in 1971, an American president broke the link with a television address.",
  [
    ["Money tied to metal", "Under the gold standard a currency could be swapped for a fixed amount of gold, which kept inflation low but tied governments' hands.",
      "Under the gold standard a government promised that its paper money could be exchanged for a fixed weight of gold on demand. Britain led the way. The pound was effectively linked to gold from the early 1700s, partly through a gold price set in 1717 while Isaac Newton was Master of the Mint, and formally from 1821. By the 1870s Germany, the United States and many others had joined. Because each currency was fixed to gold, they were fixed to each other, making trade and lending more predictable. Governments could not simply print money, so long-run inflation stayed low. The classical gold standard lasted until the First World War."],
    ["The golden straitjacket", "The money supply depended on gold, not on what the economy needed, so downturns were hard to fight.",
      "The discipline came at a cost. The money supply depended on how much gold a country held, not on what the economy needed. When gold flowed out, a country often had to raise interest rates and squeeze its economy, even in a slump. Prices could fall for years, making debts harder to repay. The system also depended on new gold discoveries. Gold rushes in California, Australia and South Africa expanded the world's money supply in ways that had nothing to do with economic policy."],
    ["Gold and the Depression", "Countries that abandoned gold earlier generally recovered from the Great Depression sooner.",
      "After the First World War, countries tried to rebuild the gold standard. In 1925 Britain, with Winston Churchill as Chancellor of the Exchequer, returned to gold at the pre-war rate, which most economists now agree overvalued the pound and hurt British industry. John Maynard Keynes attacked the decision in a pamphlet called The Economic Consequences of Mr Churchill. When the Great Depression struck, the gold standard helped spread deflation from country to country, as central banks raised rates to defend their gold. Britain left gold in 1931 and the United States effectively devalued in 1933. Research by economic historians, including Barry Eichengreen and Ben Bernanke, found that countries leaving gold earlier tended to recover faster."],
    ["Bretton Woods and Triffin", "The post-war system pegged currencies to the dollar, but its design slowly undermined faith in the gold promise.",
      "In July 1944, delegates from 44 Allied nations met at Bretton Woods, New Hampshire, led by Keynes for Britain and Harry Dexter White for the United States. Other currencies were pegged to the US dollar, and the dollar was convertible into gold at 35 dollars an ounce, but only for foreign governments and central banks. The conference also created the IMF and what became the World Bank.\n\nAround 1960, economist Robert Triffin spotted a hidden contradiction. The world needed a growing supply of dollars for trade, which came from American spending abroad. But the more dollars piled up overseas, the less likely America held enough gold to honour them. Vietnam war spending widened the gap, and France under de Gaulle began converting dollars into gold."],
    ["The Nixon shock", "In August 1971 the US suspended the dollar's convertibility into gold, ending the gold link for good.",
      "On Sunday 15 August 1971, President Richard Nixon went on television and announced that the United States would temporarily stop converting dollars into gold for foreign governments. He also imposed wage and price controls and a surcharge on imports. The suspension was never lifted. Attempts to patch together new fixed rates collapsed, and by 1973 the major currencies were floating against each other. Since then, the world's money has been fiat money, valuable because governments declare it legal tender and people trust it, not because it can be exchanged for metal. Some still call for a return to gold, but most economists argue that flexibility in crises is worth the loss of the golden anchor."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Under a gold standard, what could you do with paper money?", ["Exchange it for a fixed amount of gold","Use it only within one city","Exchange it for shares","Nothing beyond buying goods"], 0, "A fixed link to gold limited money printing and kept currencies stable against each other."],
    [1, "What was the main drawback of tying the money supply to gold?", ["Gold was too heavy to transport","The money supply followed gold holdings, not the economy's needs","It caused very high inflation","Banks could not lend at all"], 1, "A country losing gold often had to squeeze its economy even in a slump."],
    [2, "What did economic historians find about countries that left gold early in the Great Depression?", ["They suffered longer depressions","They saw no difference","They tended to recover sooner","They were shut out of trade"], 2, "Leaving gold freed central banks to ease policy instead of defending the gold link."],
    [3, "What was the Triffin dilemma?", ["Gold supplies grew faster than trade","Exchange rates changed every day","The IMF had too little money to lend","More dollars abroad meant weaker gold backing"], 3, "Supplying the world with dollars meant issuing more than America's gold could cover at 35 dollars an ounce."],
    [4, "What did President Nixon announce on 15 August 1971?", ["A return to the gold standard","A new currency","A suspension of dollar convertibility into gold","The founding of the IMF"], 2, "The suspension was never lifted, and by 1973 major currencies floated against each other."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"Money was once promised to be as good as gold. One Sunday night changed that."});

WB.L("global-financial-crisis", "money", "intermediate", "The global financial crisis of 2008",
  "It began with American home loans that should never have been made. Within months, some of the world's biggest banks were failing and governments were scrambling to stop a second Great Depression.",
  [
    ["A boom built on credit", "In the early 2000s, easy loans to risky borrowers drove US house prices up, on the assumption they would keep rising.",
      "In the early 2000s American house prices rose rapidly for years. Interest rates were low after the dot-com crash, and lenders competed fiercely for business. Many began offering subprime mortgages to borrowers with weak credit histories, sometimes with little checking of income at all, and often with low introductory rates that would jump later. The thinking was that rising house prices would bail everyone out: a struggling borrower could refinance or sell at a profit. Buyers, lenders and investors all assumed prices would keep climbing, or at least would not fall across the whole country at once. When prices peaked around 2006 and began to fall, that assumption collapsed, and defaults began to climb."],
    ["Hidden risk and leverage", "Mortgages were bundled into complex securities, and heavily borrowed banks could be wiped out by small losses.",
      "Many lenders did not keep the loans they made. Banks bundled thousands of mortgages into securities and sold them to investors, a process called securitisation. These were repackaged into more complex products, such as collateralised debt obligations, which split the income into layers of risk. Rating agencies gave many top layers their highest AAA rating. Pension funds, banks and councils worldwide bought them. Lenders who could sell loans on had less reason to care whether borrowers could repay, and few investors understood where the risk sat.\n\nLeverage magnified the danger. Many investment banks borrowed around thirty dollars or more for every dollar of their own capital, much of it short term. A small fall in asset values could wipe them out."],
    ["September 2008", "The collapse of Lehman Brothers triggered a global panic and frozen credit markets.",
      "In March 2008 the investment bank Bear Stearns was rescued through a sale to JPMorgan Chase, with support from the US Federal Reserve. Then, in September, the authorities chose not to rescue Lehman Brothers, a 158-year-old investment bank. On 15 September it filed for bankruptcy, the largest in US history, with assets of more than 600 billion dollars. Within days the insurer AIG, which had sold huge amounts of protection against mortgage defaults, needed a massive government rescue. A large money market fund that held Lehman debt ‘broke the buck’, sparking runs on similar funds. Lending between banks almost seized up, and share markets plunged. Governments responded with unprecedented measures, including the 700 billion dollar US rescue program known as TARP."],
    ["How Australia dodged recession", "Fast stimulus, sharp rate cuts, sound banks and Chinese demand helped Australia avoid a recession.",
      "Australia came through better than almost any other advanced economy. Its big banks had less exposure to American subprime securities and were closely supervised. The Reserve Bank cut the cash rate from 7.25 per cent in September 2008 to 3 per cent by April 2009. The government guaranteed bank deposits and wholesale funding, and spent heavily on stimulus, including cash payments to households and a large school building program. Meanwhile China launched its own enormous stimulus, keeping demand for Australian iron ore and coal strong. The economy shrank in only one quarter, at the end of 2008, so it avoided the technical definition of a recession: two consecutive quarters of decline."],
    ["The long shadow", "The crisis led to tougher bank rules and years of low interest rates and slow growth.",
      "Elsewhere, the damage lasted years. Millions of Americans lost their homes, US unemployment reached around 10 per cent, and parts of Europe slid into a debt crisis from 2010, with Greece the most severe case. Central banks cut interest rates to near zero and bought bonds on a vast scale. Regulators responded with new rules. The international Basel III standards required banks to hold more and better quality capital and more liquid assets, and the US passed the Dodd-Frank Act in 2010. Debate continues over whether the reforms went far enough, and over the fairness of rescuing banks while many ordinary people bore the costs. The crisis also fuelled lasting public anger at finance."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What were subprime mortgages?", ["Loans only for commercial property","Mortgages guaranteed by the government","Home loans to borrowers with weak credit histories","Loans with no interest"], 2, "Lenders assumed rising house prices would rescue risky borrowers, and that assumption failed when prices fell."],
    [1, "What is securitisation?", ["Hiring security guards for banks","Bundling loans into securities sold to investors","Insuring deposits","Raising interest rates"], 1, "Selling loans on spread the risk around the world and weakened lenders' reason to check borrowers."],
    [2, "Why did leverage make banks so vulnerable?", ["Small losses could wipe out their capital","They had too few customers","They were banned from lending","Their shares were too expensive"], 0, "With around thirty dollars borrowed for every dollar of capital, thin cushions vanished quickly."],
    [3, "What happened after Lehman Brothers filed for bankruptcy in September 2008?", ["Credit markets calmed","Bank lending almost seized up and share markets plunged","House prices began to recover","Rescues were no longer needed"], 1, "Lehman's failure turned a crisis into a global panic, forcing governments into unprecedented rescues."],
    [4, "Which of these helped Australia avoid recession during the crisis?", ["Sharp interest rate rises","Heavy holdings of US subprime debt","A collapse in Chinese demand","Fast rate cuts and government stimulus"], 3, "Sound banks, fast policy action and China's demand for Australian resources softened the blow."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"A 158-year-old bank was allowed to fail. The world's credit froze within days."});

WB.L("market-for-lemons", "money", "intermediate", "The market for lemons: when sellers know more than buyers",
  "A short paper about dodgy used cars was rejected by several journals. It went on to win a Nobel Prize and explain why some markets break down.",
  [
    ["Peaches and lemons", "When sellers know far more than buyers, good products can be driven out of a market.",
      "In the United States a defective car is called a lemon, and a good one a peach. In 1970 economist George Akerlof used used cars to explore a powerful idea. The seller knows the car's history. The buyer usually does not. Economists call this asymmetric information. If buyers cannot tell peaches from lemons, they offer only an average price. To the owner of a genuine peach, that is too low, so many decide not to sell. That leaves more lemons, buyers lower their offers further, and more good cars leave. In the extreme, the market collapses until only lemons remain, or no trade happens at all, even though buyers would gladly pay a fair price for a good car."],
    ["Rejected, then rewarded", "Akerlof's paper was turned down several times before it became a classic.",
      "The paper had a rocky start. Akerlof later recounted that several leading journals rejected it, with reviewers suggesting it was too trivial or, alternatively, that if it were correct, economics would be different. It was eventually published in the Quarterly Journal of Economics in 1970. Over time it became one of the most cited papers in the field, because the idea applied far beyond cars. In 2001 Akerlof shared the Nobel Prize in economics with Michael Spence and Joseph Stiglitz, who had developed related ideas about how markets cope with hidden information. Together their work helped launch the economics of information, which now underpins thinking about insurance, lending and online trade."],
    ["Adverse selection", "People who expect to claim are keener to buy insurance, which can push premiums up.",
      "Insurance shows the lemons problem in reverse. The buyer of health insurance often knows more about their own health than the insurer does. If premiums are set at an average level, people who expect to need lots of care find the deal attractive, while healthy people may decide it is not worth it. As the healthy leave, the insured pool gets sicker and more expensive, premiums rise and still more healthy people drop out. This spiral is called adverse selection. Australia's Lifetime Health Cover, introduced in 2000, tackles it by adding a loading to private hospital cover premiums for people who first take out cover later in life, from their early thirties on, encouraging people to join while young and healthy."],
    ["Signals and screens", "Markets fight back with warranties, credentials and checks that reveal hidden quality.",
      "Markets are not helpless. Michael Spence showed how the informed side can send a signal, an action that is costly for low-quality sellers to fake. A dealer who offers a long warranty on a used car signals confidence, since honouring it on a lemon would be expensive. Spence's best-known example was education: a degree may signal persistence and ability to employers, whatever its direct usefulness. Joseph Stiglitz showed how the less informed side can screen, for instance by offering a menu of insurance policies with different excesses, so customers reveal their own risk by what they choose. Brand reputations, independent inspections and vehicle history reports all do the same job, making hidden quality visible."],
    ["Lemons everywhere", "Much of modern commerce, from online reviews to consumer law, exists to close information gaps.",
      "Once you see the lemons problem, it appears everywhere. Online marketplaces rely on ratings and reviews so strangers can trust each other, and fake reviews are damaging precisely because they poison those signals. Lenders check credit histories because borrowers know more about their own finances. Employers ask for references. Consumer protection laws, including statutory guarantees that goods must be of acceptable quality, give buyers some assurance and reduce the advantage of sellers who hide defects. Akerlof's insight was that missing information is not a minor detail. It can stop a market working at all. Much of the machinery of modern commerce, from warranties to watchdogs, exists to close that gap."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "In Akerlof's model, why might good cars disappear from the market?", ["Buyers start to prefer older cars","The government bans them from sale","Average prices are too low for their owners","Good cars are always exported abroad"], 2, "When buyers cannot tell quality, the average price drives out the best sellers, leaving mostly lemons."],
    [1, "What is asymmetric information?", ["When prices differ between two countries","When one side of a deal knows much more than the other","When both sides share all information","When information is protected by copyright"], 1, "The gap between what sellers and buyers know is what makes the lemons problem possible."],
    [2, "What is adverse selection in insurance?", ["Insurers choosing only healthy customers","Choosing the wrong policy by mistake","A ban on insurance for young people","The likeliest claimants being keenest to buy cover"], 3, "As healthier people drop out, the pool gets costlier, which is why schemes like Lifetime Health Cover reward early joiners."],
    [3, "Why can a long warranty work as a signal?", ["Sellers are required by law to offer one","A bad car would cost the seller dearly to honour","It always lowers the car's price","It hides the car's history from buyers"], 1, "A signal works when low-quality sellers cannot afford to fake it."],
    [4, "Why do online marketplaces rely on ratings and reviews?", ["They let strangers judge quality they cannot inspect","They replace the need for prices","They are required by consumer law","They stop sellers from advertising"], 0, "Reviews close the information gap between buyers and sellers, which is why fake reviews do so much damage."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"A paper about dodgy used cars was rejected again and again. Then it won a Nobel."});

WB.L("measuring-inequality", "money", "intermediate", "Inequality: how the gap between rich and poor is measured",
  "Is the gap between rich and poor growing? The answer depends on what you measure, and economists have been arguing about it for more than a century.",
  [
    ["Income versus wealth", "Income is what flows in each year; wealth is what you own, and it is usually far more unequal.",
      "Talk about inequality often blurs two different things. Income is money that comes in over a period, from wages, business profits, interest, rent or government payments. Wealth is the stock of what you own minus what you owe: a house, superannuation, shares, savings and a car, less any mortgage or debts. A young doctor might have a high income but little wealth, while a retired farmer could have modest income and substantial wealth in land. Across almost every country, wealth is far more unequally distributed than income. Wealth builds up over a lifetime, earns its own returns and can be passed on, so differences tend to compound over time."],
    ["The Gini coefficient", "A single number from 0 to 1, drawn from the Lorenz curve, summarises how unequally income or wealth is shared.",
      "In 1905 the American economist Max Lorenz proposed a neat way to picture inequality. Line everyone up from poorest to richest and plot what share of total income the bottom 10 per cent, 20 per cent and so on receive. If everyone earned the same, the line would be perfectly straight. The more it sags, the more unequal the society. In 1912 the Italian statistician Corrado Gini turned this into a single number. A Gini coefficient of 0 means perfect equality, and 1 means one person has everything. Most developed countries have income Gini figures between roughly 0.25 and 0.40, and wealth figures that are considerably higher."],
    ["When averages mislead", "Wealth concentrates at the top in a similar pattern everywhere, so the median says more than the mean.",
      "Around 1900, Italian economist Vilfredo Pareto studied tax and property records from several countries and centuries. He found the top of the distribution followed a similar pattern again and again, with a small share of people holding a large share of the total. The popular 80/20 rule was named after him, though it is far looser than his mathematics. This power law still describes the top of wealth distributions well.\n\nIt means averages can mislead. If a billionaire walks into a pub, the average wealth soars, yet nobody else is richer. Economists often prefer the median, the person exactly in the middle, to the mean. They also compare the 90th and 10th percentiles, or track the top 1 per cent's income share."],
    ["Kuznets, Piketty and debate", "Economists disagree about whether growth naturally narrows or widens inequality.",
      "In 1955 economist Simon Kuznets suggested that inequality rises as countries industrialise and then falls as they mature, an upside-down U called the Kuznets curve. The mid-twentieth century seemed to prove him right, as gaps narrowed in many rich countries. Then, from around 1980, top income shares rose again in the United States, Britain and elsewhere. In 2013 French economist Thomas Piketty published Capital in the Twenty-First Century, arguing that when the return on capital exceeds the growth rate of the economy, wealth tends to concentrate. His book became a surprise bestseller and drew strong criticism, with economists still debating his data, his theory and his proposed wealth taxes."],
    ["Can you climb the ladder?", "How easily people move up or down the income ladder matters as much as the size of the gap.",
      "A snapshot of inequality does not say whether the same people stay at the top or bottom. Economic mobility asks how strongly your adult income is linked to your parents' income. Research led by economist Miles Corak found that countries with higher inequality tend to have lower mobility between generations, a link dubbed the Great Gatsby curve by Alan Krueger in 2012. It is a correlation, and researchers debate what drives it, from education and housing to social networks. Measured mobility in Australia has generally been found to be higher than in the United States, though comparing countries is tricky. Many people care less about the gap itself than about whether the ladder is climbable."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why is wealth usually more unequal than income?", ["Wealth is never taxed","It compounds over time and can be inherited","Income is set by law in most countries","Wealth only counts cash holdings"], 1, "Wealth compounds over time and passes between generations, so gaps widen."],
    [1, "What does a Gini coefficient of 0 mean?", ["One person has everything","The data are missing","The economy is not growing","Everyone has exactly the same"], 3, "The Gini runs from 0, perfect equality, to 1, where one person holds everything."],
    [2, "Why do economists often prefer the median to the mean when describing typical wealth?", ["A few very rich people can inflate the mean","Medians are required in official reports","The mean cannot be worked out for wealth","The median always rises faster over time"], 0, "One billionaire in a pub lifts the average without making anyone else richer; the median is unmoved."],
    [3, "What did Thomas Piketty argue in Capital in the Twenty-First Century?", ["Inequality always falls as economies grow richer","Money should be fully backed by gold reserves","Wealth concentrates when capital outgrows the economy","Inequality is too hard to measure reliably"], 2, "If capital grows faster than the economy, those who own it pull ahead, though economists still debate his case."],
    [4, "What does the Great Gatsby curve describe?", ["Rising house prices in New York","The spending habits of the very rich","The shape of the Lorenz curve","Higher inequality tied to lower mobility"], 3, "Where gaps are larger, children's incomes tend to depend more on their parents' incomes, though the causes are debated."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"Why a billionaire walking into a pub makes the average drinker look richer."});

WB.L("quantitative-easing", "money", "advanced", "Quantitative easing: what central banks do when rates hit zero",
  "When interest rates can fall no further, central banks reach for a stranger tool: creating money to buy bonds. Australia tried it in 2020, with mixed results.",
  [
    ["The zero lower bound", "Central banks steer the economy with interest rates, but cutting them stops working once they approach zero.",
      "Normally the Reserve Bank of Australia steers the economy with its cash rate, the overnight rate at which banks lend to each other. Cutting it makes borrowing cheaper and encourages spending and investment. But there is a floor. Near zero, further cuts become difficult, because people and banks could simply hold cash, which pays nothing but never charges you either. Economists call this the zero lower bound. A few central banks in Europe and Japan pushed rates slightly negative, but not by much. When a severe downturn hits and rates are already near zero, central banks need another tool. The best known is quantitative easing, or QE."],
    ["How QE works", "A central bank creates new reserves to buy bonds, which pushes longer-term interest rates down.",
      "Under QE, a central bank buys large amounts of financial assets, mainly government bonds, from banks and investors. It pays with newly created reserves: electronic money in the accounts that commercial banks hold at the central bank. Despite the popular phrase, no banknotes are printed. The buying pushes bond prices up, and because prices and yields move in opposite directions, longer-term interest rates fall. That feeds through to mortgages and business loans. Sellers also receive cash they may put into corporate bonds or shares, an effect called portfolio rebalancing. QE also signals that the bank intends to keep rates low for a long time."],
    ["From Tokyo to the world", "Japan pioneered QE in 2001, and other major central banks adopted it after the 2008 financial crisis.",
      "Japan tried modern QE first. After its asset bubble burst in the early 1990s, it endured years of weak growth and falling prices. From 2001 to 2006 the Bank of Japan bought government bonds to expand bank reserves, and it later revived the approach on a far larger scale. The 2008 financial crisis made QE mainstream. The US Federal Reserve bought trillions of dollars of bonds and mortgage-backed securities, the Bank of England began in 2009 and the European Central Bank in 2015. Related tools exist too. The Fed capped bond yields from the Second World War until 1951, and the Bank of Japan adopted a version in 2016."],
    ["Australia's 2020 experiment", "The RBA bought bonds and targeted yields in 2020, then abandoned its yield target abruptly in 2021.",
      "Australia skipped QE in the global financial crisis because its rates were well above zero. The pandemic changed that. In March 2020 the RBA cut the cash rate to 0.25 per cent, set the same target for three-year government bond yields and offered banks cheap funding. In November 2020 it cut both to 0.1 per cent and began a $100 billion Bond Purchase Program, later extended to roughly $280 billion in total.\n\nThe yield target ended badly. In 2021 the recovery was faster than expected and inflation rose, so markets bet on earlier rate rises than the bank's guidance of low rates until 2024. The RBA abandoned the target in November 2021, and later reviews acknowledged the disorderly exit damaged its credibility."],
    ["Did it work?", "QE probably helped steady markets in crises, but its wider effects are hard to measure and its side effects are debated.",
      "Most central bank research finds QE lowered long-term interest rates and helped steady markets in crises. Its effect on jobs and growth is much harder to measure, because nobody can rerun history without it. Critics raise three concerns. By lifting the prices of bonds, shares and property, QE may have widened wealth gaps, since wealthier people own most of those assets. It can blur the line between monetary policy and government financing. And when rates later rose, central banks faced large losses on the bonds they held. The RBA has reported negative equity as a result, though central banks can keep operating normally in that situation."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why do central banks struggle to cut interest rates much below zero?", ["Banks refuse to lend at very low rates","Holding cash pays nothing but costs nothing","Governments ban negative rates by law","Cutting rates always triggers immediate inflation"], 1, "Near zero, holding cash becomes a rival to deposits, so further cuts lose their force."],
    [1, "How does buying bonds under QE push longer-term interest rates down?", ["The central bank raises the cash rate to offset it","Newly printed banknotes flood into mortgages","The purchases lift bond prices, and higher prices mean lower yields","Banks are required to cut their loan rates"], 2, "Bond prices and yields move in opposite directions, so heavy buying lowers longer-term rates."],
    [2, "How did QE change after the 2008 financial crisis?", ["Japan and the US abandoned it for good","It was limited to Australia","It was replaced by deeply negative rates everywhere","It spread from Japan to become a standard tool of major central banks"], 3, "An emergency experiment became a normal part of the central bank toolkit."],
    [3, "Why did the RBA's yield target collapse in 2021?", ["Markets expected rate rises sooner than the RBA signalled","Banks refused to buy any more government bonds","The RBA ran out of reserves it could create","Federal government debt had been fully repaid"], 0, "Markets bet against the RBA's guidance, and the bank dropped the target, which damaged its credibility."],
    [4, "Why do critics say QE may have widened wealth gaps?", ["It sharply cut wages for lower-paid workers","It lifted asset prices, which wealthier people mostly own","It raised taxes on ordinary household savings","It gave newly created money directly to banks' owners"], 1, "Higher asset prices mainly benefit those who already own the assets."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"Central banks can create money to buy bonds. Why isn't that just printing cash?"});

